WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.250
Our science club built a weather

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.750
agent, and it crashed on day one.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.560
The model asked for a tool called bus.

4
00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:10.550
There was no bus tool, and

5
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.050
nobody had planned for that!

6
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.750
Today: tools as Python functions,

7
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.750
and how to call the right one.

8
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.180
In your agent, every tool is

9
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.420
just a Python function.

10
00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.170
It takes the call’s

11
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.330
details as a dictionary.

12
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.950
It looks something up, or does something.

13
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.140
And it returns a result,

14
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.420
as a dictionary too.

15
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.650
Our tools work over a tiny made-up

16
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.190
world: two villages and their weather,

17
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.750
one class and its timetable.

18
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.290
Darsi Tiny’s ten tools work the same

19
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.610
way, over a bigger made-up school.

20
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.120
Nothing real is looked up,

21
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.680
and nothing is sent.

22
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.210
Dispatch means sending a call

23
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.090
to the right function.

24
00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.490
Keep a dictionary from each

25
00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.330
tool’s name to its function.

26
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.690
Then TOOLS, at the name, is the function,

27
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.750
and you call it with the details.

28
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.330
Models make mistakes, so

29
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.190
tools get strange calls.

30
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.340
A tool that isn’t there, like bus.

31
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.060
A missing detail, like no place.

32
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.150
A place that doesn’t exist, like Moonvale.

33
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.480
Each one should come back as

34
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.000
an error, not a crash.

35
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.700
So your run function checks first.

36
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.910
If the name isn’t in TOOLS, it

37
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.310
returns an error dictionary.

38
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.300
The model reads that error like

39
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.540
any other result, and tells the

40
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:42.580
person it can’t help.

41
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.270
Quick check!

42
00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.250
The model calls the attendance tool, but

43
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.030
your TOOLS has only weather and timetable.

44
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.720
What should run do?

45
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.420
Return an error result.

46
00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.580
A crash stops the whole agent.

47
00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.250
Guessing another tool is worse: it

48
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.950
does something nobody asked for.

49
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.090
An error lets the model say:

50
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.290
sorry, I can’t check attendance.

51
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.000
Last step: the result goes

52
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.060
back to the model.

53
00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.170
But the model reads only text.

54
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.630
So json dot dumps turns the

55
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.750
dictionary back into JSON text, and

56
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.290
the loop adds it to the history.

57
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.930
In the lab, Darsi Tiny’s ten

58
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.170
tools are switches.

59
00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.600
Turn the weather tool off,

60
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.980
and ask for the weather.

61
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.020
Ask for something no tool can do.

62
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.600
Then ask about class nine Z, and

63
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.960
read the check under the call.

64
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:42.900
Your turn.

65
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.700
The model calls the weather tool,

66
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.200
but leaves out the place.

67
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.670
What should the weather tool return?

68
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.170
An error that says the place is missing.

69
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.310
Remember: each tool is a function,

70
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.550
from details to a result.

71
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.230
A dictionary of tools sends

72
00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.710
each call to the right one.

73
00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.630
And every mistake comes back as

74
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.290
an error, not a crash.

75
00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.030
Try it yourself: invent a third

76
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.150
tool, maybe a library search.

77
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:17.150
On paper, write its details, its result,

78
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.470
and two errors it should return.

79
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.500
Then add it to TOOLS, and test it.
